# cm_mcp_engine configuration.
#
# Copy to .env. The engine serves the pinned registry against the real upstream
# its contracts name, so a usable .env needs DEV_OFFLINE=0 and a credential.
# Nothing here is required to run the tests.
#
#   var                  default            what it decides
#   -------------------  -----------------  ------------------------------------
#   CM_REGISTRY_FILE     (unset)            override: a registry built elsewhere
#   CM_CONTRACTS_DIR     (unset)            override: a bare directory of contracts
#   DEV_OFFLINE          1                  TEST ONLY -- see section 2
#   SALLA_ACCESS_TOKEN   (unset)            the credential for api: "salla"
#   CM_PRINCIPAL         local-dev          who calls are made on behalf of
#   MCP_HOST             127.0.0.1          where the MCP server listens
#   MCP_PORT             8765                 "
#   MOCK_API_PORT        8787               TEST ONLY -- the suite's own upstream


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. Which contracts this engine serves
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE RULE: this engine reads its own repository. With neither variable below set
# it serves ./registry/registry.json -- the registry the pipeline published,
# verified, and a human merged here. It does not look in a contracts checkout, and
# there is no fallback that quietly finds one.
#
# The engine prints the source it resolved at startup and reports it from
# `list_contracts`, because a catalog that looks wrong is almost always a source
# that is not what you assumed.
#
# Two overrides, both deliberate, both reported as unapproved:
#
#   CM_REGISTRY_FILE   a registry index built elsewhere. To iterate on a contract
#                      without publishing it, build one and point this at it:
#                        cd ../cm_mcp_contracts
#                        uv run python scripts/build_registry.py --out ../reg
#                      then CM_REGISTRY_FILE=../reg/registry.json
#
#   CM_CONTRACTS_DIR   a plain directory of contract files -- no index, so no
#                      provenance and no hashes. This repo's tests use it against
#                      pinned fixtures.
#
# CM_REGISTRY_FILE=
# CM_CONTRACTS_DIR=


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. Real upstreams (DEV_OFFLINE is for the test suite)
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Set this to 0. A running engine -- local or deployed -- calls the real APIs its
# contracts name; the credential below is then required, and stale generated code
# is retired automatically, because the cache identity covers the resolved host
# and not just the contract.
#
# 1 resolves every upstream to tests/mock_upstream.py instead of the internet. It
# exists so the suite can exercise http bindings without a token or a network, and
# tests/conftest.py sets it for itself. The mock is a test fixture and ships in no
# distribution, so setting this to 1 outside the suite buys nothing: there is no
# simulator running to answer, and the calls simply fail to connect.
#
# The point of it is that the mock speaks the same envelope, pagination and error
# shape as the upstream, so the generated code is identical either way.
DEV_OFFLINE=0


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3. Credentials, one per upstream
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The UPSTREAMS table in cm_engine/config.py maps a contract's `api` name to a
# host and to the variable named here. A contract names an upstream and never a
# token, so credentials live in this file and only in this file -- never in a
# contract, never in the registry, never in generated code, which carries the
# variable's NAME and reads the value from the sandbox environment at call time.
#
# Unset is fine while DEV_OFFLINE=1: the mock accepts any token, and the engine
# says on stdout that it is sending a placeholder.
#
# Adding an upstream to that table means adding its token variable here too.

# api: "salla" -- a credential the Salla Admin API accepts for the store.
#
# Not every Salla token is one. A dashboard login token decodes to `source: 2fa`
# with a user and a device and no scope claim, and the Admin API answers 401 --
# which the engine reports using the contract's own 401 sentence, so the message
# reads like a missing scope rather than the wrong kind of token.
#
# Expires, and nothing here refreshes it.
#
# If the credential you hold authenticates against a different host, that is a
# three-line entry in the UPSTREAMS table in cm_engine/config.py, and no contract
# changes -- which is the point of keeping hosts here.
# SALLA_ACCESS_TOKEN=


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 4. Who calls are made on behalf of
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The principal scopes both the credential lookup and the result cache, so two
# merchants asking the same question can never read each other's answers. This
# POC serves one store; a deployment maps an authenticated MCP session to the
# merchant install behind it in `_principal()` in cm_engine/server.py, and this
# variable stops mattering.
#
# It is set here and never accepted as a tool argument -- those are written by a
# language model, so a prompt could otherwise choose whose data comes back.
# CM_PRINCIPAL=local-dev


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 5. Where things listen
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# cm_mcp_agent reaches the engine at MCP_HOST:MCP_PORT/mcp -- change it here and
# change CM_MCP_URL in that repo's .env to match.
MCP_HOST=127.0.0.1
MCP_PORT=8765

# The suite's in-process upstream. Read only when DEV_OFFLINE=1, which outside the
# tests is never -- conftest.py picks a free port and sets this for itself, so
# nothing here needs to hold 8787 for it.
# MOCK_API_PORT=8787
